<TopicPage slug="subjects/gender-studies/foundational/sex-gender-and-sexuality-the-vocabulary">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Gender studies begins by taking apart a word everyone uses daily: "gender". The field's first
    move is analytic — separating sex (biology), gender (social meanings and roles) and sexuality
    (desire and orientation) — not to settle debates by definition, but to make sharper questions
    possible: which differences track bodies, and which track societies?
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the foundational distinctions" widget="equationStep" config={vocabCfg} />

  <h2>The evidence for construction</h2>
  <p>
    If gender roles were natural law, they would be constant. Instead, the historical and
    anthropological record shows enormous variety: which work counts as men's or women's, what
    dress codes mean, how many genders a society recognises — some cultures have long
    institutionalised more than two. Variation is not proof of no biology; it is proof that biology
    does not dictate the meanings.
  </p>

  <h2>From vocabulary to analysis</h2>
  <p>
    The distinctions earn their keep immediately. The wage gap, for example, decomposes into parts:
    differences in hours, in occupations, in caregiving interruptions, and in pay for the same work
    — each with a different remedy. "Gender pay gap" is not one problem but a bundle, and the
    sex/gender distinction is the tool that un-bundles it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the field goes from here</h2>
  <p>
    From this vocabulary the discipline unfolds historically (the suffrage and liberation
    movements), theoretically (liberal, radical, intersectional and queer theory), and
    institutionally (work, media, law, family). The through-line is constant: gender is a lens on
    power — who gets to be what, and who pays for it — and its analysis is empirical as well as
    political.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NWSA programme guidelines; Butler, <em>Gender Trouble</em>; Lorber, <em>Paradoxes of Gender</em>; Fausto-Sterling, <em>Sexing the Body</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
